StringToU16 returns UTF-16, so say char16_t

The function decodes UTF-8 into UTF-16 code units, pairs surrogates by hand and is read
back against `u'...'` literals, but it collected the result in a `std::wstring`. `wchar_t`
is 32 bits on Linux and macOS, so that container was never the width the name claims;
`char16_t` is.

libc++ here is built without wide characters, having no locale to convert through, so
`std::wstring` does not exist and the file did not compile. `std::u16string` needs none of
that.
